<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huibin Jang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1659">1659</a>–<a href="/years/1701">1701</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Concubine Hui, Jang Ok-jeong, Lady Jang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huibin Jang, personal name Jang Ok-jeong, was a consort of King Sukjong of Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and the mother of King Gyeongjong. Born into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chungin">jungin</a> class from a long line of interpreters and tied to the Namin faction, she entered the palace as a lady-in-waiting and rose through the ranks of royal consort from 1686, bearing the king's first son in 1688. She was made queen in 1689, when Queen Min was deposed, but lost the king's favour and was demoted in 1694. Accused of cursing Queen Min, who died in 1701, she was executed by poisoning that year, and Sukjong forbade any royal consort from becoming queen.</p></div>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eunpyeong District, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in Changgyeonggung, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.6,126.93&amp;year=1686" aria-label="Eunpyeong District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7721 811 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Huibin Jang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8171 1081h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8171 1081h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.6,126.93&amp;year=1686">What was here in 1686? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
